        {
          "path": "_m/g/c/u/v/tron-theatre-wp/skull-a-companion-piece-to-che.jpg",
          "filename": "\"Skull\" (a companion piece to \"Cherub\") - geograph.org.uk - 1015330.jpg",
          "caption": "\"Skull\" (a companion piece to \"Cherub\"). A pair of bronze sculptures, powder-coated with gold, can be seen on opposite corners of the Tron Theatre. Together, they form a single work: Cherub/Skull. \"Cherub\" was installed first; of the two, it has the more prominent location, and can be seen here: 938201.\nThe other half of this work is \"Skull\"; it was installed in the winter of 1998, and is located in a much quieter street. (For a different view of the skull, see: 1055858.)\n\nBoth works are by Kenny Hunter and were cast by Powderhall Bronze (for more works by the same artist, see: 949393, 948674, 546448, 305846, and 1069028).",
          "alt": "\"Skull\" (a companion piece to \"Cherub\"). A pair of bronze sculptures, powder-coated with gold, can be seen on opposite corners of the Tron Theatre. Together, they form a single work: Cherub/Skull. \"Cherub\" was installed first; of the two, it has the more prominent location, and can be seen here: 938201.\nThe other half of this work is \"Skull\"; it was installed in the winter of 1998, and is located in a much quieter street. (For a different view of the skull, see: 1055858.)\n\nBoth works are by Kenny Hunter and were cast by Powderhall Bronze (for more works by the same artist, see: 949393, 948674, 546448, 305846, and 1069028).",
          "width": 640,
          "height": 480,
